 780| ##### throwHttpErrors
 781| 
 782| Type: `boolean | (status: number) => boolean`\
 783| Default: `true`
 784| 
 785| Throw an `HTTPError` when, after following redirects, the response has a non-2xx status code. To also throw for redirects instead of following them, set the [`redirect`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WindowOrWorkerGlobalScope/fetch#Parameters) option to `'manual'`.
 786| 
 787| Setting this to `false` may be useful if you are checking for resource availability and are expecting error responses.
 788| 
 789| You can also pass a function that accepts the HTTP status code and returns a boolean for selective error handling. Note that this can violate the principle of least surprise, so it's recommended to use the boolean form unless you have a specific use case like treating 404 responses differently.
 790| 
 791| Note: If `false`, error responses are considered successful and the request will not be retried.
 792| 
 793| Note: [Opaque responses](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Response/type) from `no-cors` requests are returned as-is (without throwing `HTTPError`), since the actual status is hidden by the browser.
 794| 
 795| ##### onDownloadProgress
 796| 
 797| Type: `Function`
 798| 
 799| Download progress event handler.
 800| 
 801| The function receives these arguments:
 802| - `progress` is an object with these properties:
 803|   - `percent` is a number between 0 and 1 representing the progress percentage.
 804|   - `transferredBytes` is the number of bytes transferred so far.
 805|   - `totalBytes` is the total number of bytes to be transferred. This is an estimate and may be 0 for an empty transfer or when the total size cannot be determined.
 806| - `chunk` is an instance of `Uint8Array` containing the data that was received. When an empty response body stream completes, the callback receives an empty chunk.
 807| 
 808| `content-length` is only used as the total for a response that is not content-coded, since it counts encoded bytes while the progress stream counts the bytes after decompression. A compressed response therefore reports `totalBytes: 0` until it completes, so the percentage cannot be calculated while downloading. For a cross-origin response in a browser, `content-encoding` is only visible when the server lists it in `Access-Control-Expose-Headers`. Otherwise Ky cannot tell that the response is compressed, so it uses `content-length` and the percentage stays close to 100% for most of the download.
 809| 
 810| Responses with no body at all are not streamed, so no progress events are emitted for them. That covers a [null body status](https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#null-body-status) such as `204`, and a `HEAD` response in runtimes that give it no body, such as browsers, Node.js and Deno. Bun gives a `HEAD` response an empty body, so it reports one final event with `transferredBytes: 0`. A response whose body an `afterResponse` hook already read, or locked with a reader, is passed through unchanged, so it reports no progress either.
 811| 
 812| Do not throw from the callback. A throw fails the response stream, which the runtime reports in its own way: Node.js rejects the body read with your error, while Chromium reports a network failure, so Ky's body methods throw a `NetworkError` there. Pass a `signal` from an `AbortController` to cancel instead.
 813| 
 814| ```js
 815| import ky from 'ky';
 816| 
 817| const response = await ky('https://example.com', {
 818| 	onDownloadProgress: (progress, chunk) => {
 819| 		// Example output:
 820| 		// `100% - 1271 of 1271 bytes`
 821| 		console.log(`${progress.percent * 100}% - ${progress.transferredBytes} of ${progress.totalBytes} bytes`);
 822| 	}
 823| });
 824| ```
 825| 
 826| ##### onUploadProgress
 827| 
 828| Type: `Function`
 829| 
 830| Upload progress event handler.
 831| 
 832| > [!NOTE]
 833| > Requires [request stream support](https://caniuse.com/wf-fetch-request-streams) and, in Chromium-based browsers, an HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 connection (streaming uploads over HTTP/1.1 fail with a network error, even over plain HTTP). This handler is silently ignored in unsupported environments and for requests with `keepalive: true` or `mode: 'no-cors'`, since they cannot use streaming request bodies. If an upload fails over HTTP/1.1 in Chromium, you can retry it without upload progress by returning `{options: {onUploadProgress: undefined}}` from a [`beforeRetry`](#hooksbeforeretry) hook.
 834| 
 835| The function receives these arguments:
 836| - `progress` is an object with these properties:
 837|   - `percent` is a number between 0 and 1 representing the progress percentage.
 838|   - `transferredBytes` is the number of bytes transferred so far.
 839|   - `totalBytes` is the total number of bytes to be transferred. This is an estimate and may be 0 for an empty transfer or when the total size cannot be determined.
 840| - `chunk` is an instance of `Uint8Array` containing the data that was sent. When an empty request body stream completes, the callback receives an empty chunk.
 841| 
 842| A `ReadableStream` body cannot be measured, so `totalBytes` falls back to a `content-length` header you set. Browsers do not allow that header on a request, so there the total stays `0`. When a hook replaces the request, `totalBytes` is still estimated from the `body` option until the final event, which reports the real total.
 843| 
 844| Do not throw from the callback. A throw fails the upload stream, which the runtime reports as a network failure, so Ky throws a `NetworkError` (in Node.js, with your error in its `cause` chain) and retries it like any other network error, running the callback again. Pass a `signal` from an `AbortController` to cancel instead.
 845| 
 846| ```js
 847| import ky from 'ky';
 848| 
 849| const response = await ky.post('https://example.com/upload', {
 850| 	body: largeFile,
 851| 	onUploadProgress: (progress, chunk) => {
 852| 		// Example output:
 853| 		// `100% - 1271 of 1271 bytes`
 854| 		console.log(`${progress.percent * 100}% - ${progress.transferredBytes} of ${progress.totalBytes} bytes`);
 855| 	}
 856| });
 857| ```
