 859| ##### parseJson
 860| 
 861| Type: `Function`\
 862| Default: `JSON.parse()`
 863| 
 864| User-defined JSON-parsing function.
 865| 
 866| The function receives the response text as the first argument and a context object as the second argument containing the `request` ([`Request`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Request)) and `response` ([`Response`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Response)).
 867| 
 868| Use-cases:
 869| 1. Parse JSON via the [`bourne` package](https://github.com/hapijs/bourne) to protect from prototype pollution.
 870| 2. Parse JSON with [`reviver` option of `JSON.parse()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/JSON/parse).
 871| 3. Log or handle JSON parse errors with request context.
 872| 
 873| ```js
 874| import ky from 'ky';
 875| import bourne from '@hapijs/bourne';
 876| 
 877| const json = await ky('https://example.com', {
 878| 	parseJson: text => bourne(text)
 879| }).json();
 880| ```
 881| 
 882| ```js
 883| import ky from 'ky';
 884| 
 885| const json = await ky('https://example.com', {
 886| 	parseJson: (text, {request, response}) => {
 887| 		console.log(`Parsing JSON from ${request.url} (status: ${response.status})`);
 888| 		return JSON.parse(text);
 889| 	}
 890| }).json();
 891| ```
