 918| ##### fetch
 919| 
 920| Type: `Function`\
 921| Default: `fetch`
 922| 
 923| User-defined `fetch` function.
 924| Has to be fully compatible with the [Fetch API](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Fetch_API) standard. It must resolve with a `Response`, or a `TypeError` is thrown.
 925| 
 926| Use-cases:
 927| 1. Use the `fetch` wrapper function provided by some frameworks that use server-side rendering (SSR).
 928| 2. Add custom instrumentation or logging to all requests.
 929| 
 930| ```js
 931| import ky from 'ky';
 932| 
 933| const api = ky.create({
 934| 	fetch: async (request, init) => {
 935| 		const start = performance.now();
 936| 		const response = await fetch(request, init);
 937| 		const duration = performance.now() - start;
 938| 		console.log(`${request.method} ${request.url} - ${response.status} (${Math.round(duration)}ms)`);
 939| 		return response;
 940| 	}
 941| });
 942| 
 943| const json = await api('https://example.com').json();
 944| ```
 945| 
 946| ##### context
 947| 
 948| Type: `object<string, unknown>`\
 949| Default: `{}`
 950| 
 951| User-defined data passed to hooks.
 952| 
 953| This option allows you to pass arbitrary contextual data to hooks without polluting the request itself. The context is available in all hooks and is **guaranteed to always be an object** (never `undefined`), so you can safely access properties without optional chaining.
 954| 
 955| Use cases:
 956| - Pass authentication tokens or API keys to hooks
 957| - Attach request metadata for logging or debugging
 958| - Implement conditional logic in hooks based on the request context
 959| - Pass serverless environment bindings (e.g., Cloudflare Workers)
 960| 
 961| **Note:** Context is shallow merged. Top-level properties are merged, but nested objects are replaced. Only enumerable properties are copied.
 962| 
 963| ```js
 964| import ky from 'ky';
 965| 
 966| // Pass data to hooks
 967| const api = ky.create({
 968| 	hooks: {
 969| 		beforeRequest: [
 970| 			({request, options}) => {
 971| 				const {token} = options.context;
 972| 				if (token) {
 973| 					request.headers.set('Authorization', `Bearer ${token}`);
 974| 				}
 975| 			}
 976| 		]
 977| 	}
 978| });
 979| 
 980| await api('https://example.com', {
 981| 	context: {
 982| 		token: 'secret123'
 983| 	}
 984| }).json();
 985| 
 986| // Shallow merge: only top-level properties are merged
 987| const instance = ky.create({
 988| 	context: {
 989| 		a: 1,
 990| 		b: {
 991| 			nested: true
 992| 		}
 993| 	}
 994| });
 995| 
 996| const extended = instance.extend({
 997| 	context: {
 998| 		b: {
 999| 			updated: true
1000| 		},
1001| 		c: 3
1002| 	}
1003| });
1004| // Result: {a: 1, b: {updated: true}, c: 3}
1005| // Note: The original `b.nested` is gone (shallow merge)
1006| ```
1007| 
1008| ### ky.extend(defaultOptions)
1009| 
1010| Create a new `ky` instance with some defaults overridden with your own.
1011| 
1012| In contrast to `ky.create()`, `ky.extend()` inherits defaults from its parent.
1013| 
1014| You can pass headers as a `Headers` instance or a plain object. Headers are normalized to a plain object with lowercase names when options are merged, so `init` hooks start with a plain object with lowercase keys. A header removed with `undefined` stays in that object with an `undefined` value.
1015| 
1016| You can remove a header with `.extend()` by passing the header with an `undefined` value.
1017| Passing `undefined` as a string removes the header only if it comes from a `Headers` instance.
1018| 
1019| Similarly, you can remove existing `hooks` entries by extending the hook with an explicit `undefined`.
1020| 
1021| ```js
1022| import ky from 'ky';
1023| 
1024| const url = 'https://sindresorhus.com';
1025| 
1026| const original = ky.create({
1027| 	headers: {
1028| 		rainbow: 'rainbow',
1029| 		unicorn: 'unicorn'
1030| 	},
1031| 	hooks: {
1032| 		beforeRequest: [ () => console.log('before 1') ],
1033| 		afterResponse: [ () => console.log('after 1') ],
1034| 	},
1035| });
1036| 
