1453| ## Tips
1454| 
1455| ### Sending form data
1456| 
1457| Sending form data in Ky is identical to `fetch`. Just pass a [`FormData`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/FormData) instance to the `body` option. The `Content-Type` header will be automatically set to `multipart/form-data`, unless you set a `Content-Type` in the `headers` option, which always takes precedence.
1458| 
1459| ```js
1460| import ky from 'ky';
1461| 
1462| // `multipart/form-data`
1463| const formData = new FormData();
1464| formData.append('food', 'fries');
1465| formData.append('drink', 'icetea');
1466| 
1467| const response = await ky.post(url, {body: formData});
1468| ```
1469| 
1470| If you want to send the data in `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` format, you will need to encode the data with [`URLSearchParams`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams). Like `FormData`, the `Content-Type` is set for you unless you set one in the `headers` option.
1471| 
1472| ```js
1473| import ky from 'ky';
1474| 
1475| // `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`
1476| const searchParams = new URLSearchParams();
1477| searchParams.set('food', 'fries');
1478| searchParams.set('drink', 'icetea');
1479| 
1480| const response = await ky.post(url, {body: searchParams});
1481| ```
1482| 
1483| #### Modifying FormData in hooks
1484| 
1485| If you need to modify FormData in a `beforeRequest` hook (for example, to transform field names), delete the `Content-Type` header before creating a new `Request`:
1486| 
1487| ```js
1488| import ky from 'ky';
1489| 
1490| const response = await ky.post(url, {
1491| 	body: formData,
1492| 	hooks: {
1493| 		beforeRequest: [
1494| 			({request}) => {
1495| 				const newFormData = new FormData();
1496| 
1497| 				// Modify FormData as needed
1498| 				for (const [key, value] of formData) {
1499| 					newFormData.set(key.toLowerCase(), value);
1500| 				}
1501| 
1502| 				// Delete `Content-Type` to let Request regenerate it with correct boundary
1503| 				request.headers.delete('content-type');
1504| 
1505| 				return new Request(request, {body: newFormData});
1506| 			}
1507| 		]
1508| 	}
1509| });
1510| ```
1511| 
1512| ### Setting a custom `Content-Type`
1513| 
1514| Ky automatically sets an appropriate [`Content-Type`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Headers/Content-Type) header for each request based on the data in the request body. However, some APIs require custom, non-standard content types, such as `application/x-amz-json-1.1`. Using the `headers` option, you can manually override the content type.
1515| 
1516| ```js
1517| import ky from 'ky';
1518| 
1519| const json = await ky.post('https://example.com', {
1520| 	headers: {
1521| 		'content-type': 'application/x-amz-json-1.1'
1522| 	},
1523| 	json: {
1524| 		foo: true
1525| 	},
1526| }).json();
1527| 
1528| console.log(json);
1529| //=> {data: '🦄'}
1530| ```
1531| 
1532| ### Cancellation
1533| 
1534| Fetch (and hence Ky) has built-in support for request cancellation through the [`AbortController` API](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AbortController). [Read more.](https://developers.google.com/web/updates/2017/09/abortable-fetch)
1535| 
1536| Example:
1537| 
1538| ```js
1539| import ky from 'ky';
1540| 
1541| const controller = new AbortController();
1542| const {signal} = controller;
1543| 
1544| setTimeout(() => {
1545| 	controller.abort();
1546| }, 5000);
1547| 
1548| try {
1549| 	console.log(await ky(url, {signal}).text());
1550| } catch (error) {
1551| 	if (error.name === 'AbortError') {
1552| 		console.log('Fetch aborted');
1553| 	} else {
1554| 		console.error('Fetch error:', error);
1555| 	}
1556| }
1557| ```
1558| 
1559| ### Proxy support (Node.js)
1560| 
1561| #### Native proxy support
1562| 
1563| Node.js 24.5+ supports automatic proxy configuration via environment variables. Set `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` or use the `--use-env-proxy` CLI flag.
1564| 
1565| ```sh
1566| NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1 HTTP_PROXY=http://proxy.example.com:8080 node app.js
1567| ```
1568| 
1569| Or:
1570| 
1571| ```sh
1572| node --use-env-proxy app.js
1573| ```
1574| 
1575| Supported environment variables:
1576| - `HTTP_PROXY` / `http_proxy`: Proxy URL for HTTP requests
1577| - `HTTPS_PROXY` / `https_proxy`: Proxy URL for HTTPS requests
1578| - `NO_PROXY` / `no_proxy`: Comma-separated list of hosts to bypass the proxy
1579| 
1580| #### Using ProxyAgent
1581| 
1582| For more control, use `ProxyAgent` or `EnvHttpProxyAgent` with the `dispatcher` option.
1583| 
1584| ```js
1585| import ky from 'ky';
1586| import {ProxyAgent} from 'undici';
1587| 
1588| const proxyAgent = new ProxyAgent('http://proxy.example.com:8080');
1589| 
1590| const response = await ky('https://example.com', {
1591| 	dispatcher: proxyAgent
1592| }).json();
1593| ```
1594| 
1595| Using `EnvHttpProxyAgent` to automatically read proxy settings from environment variables:
1596| 
1597| ```js
1598| import ky from 'ky';
1599| import {EnvHttpProxyAgent} from 'undici';
1600| 
1601| const proxyAgent = new EnvHttpProxyAgent();
1602| 
1603| const api = ky.extend({
1604| 	dispatcher: proxyAgent
1605| });
1606| 
1607| const response = await api('https://example.com').json();
1608| ```
1609| 
1610| ### HTTP/2 support (Node.js)
1611| 
1612| Undici supports HTTP/2, but it's not enabled by default. Create a custom dispatcher with the `allowH2` option:
1613| 
1614| ```js
1615| import ky from 'ky';
1616| import {Agent, Pool} from 'undici';
1617| 
1618| const agent = new Agent({
1619| 	factory(origin, options) {
1620| 		return new Pool(origin, {
1621| 			...options,
1622| 			allowH2: true
1623| 		});
1624| 	}
1625| });
1626| 
1627| const response = await ky('https://example.com', {
1628| 	dispatcher: agent
1629| }).json();
1630| ```
1631| 
1632| Combine proxy and HTTP/2:
1633| 
1634| ```js
1635| import ky from 'ky';
1636| import {ProxyAgent} from 'undici';
1637| 
1638| const proxyAgent = new ProxyAgent({
1639| 	uri: 'http://proxy.example.com:8080',
1640| 	allowH2: true
1641| });
1642| 
1643| const response = await ky('https://example.com', {
1644| 	dispatcher: proxyAgent
1645| }).json();
1646| ```
1647| 
1648| ### Streaming request bodies
1649| 
1650| You can pass a [`ReadableStream`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/ReadableStream) as the request body. Ky sets the `duplex: 'half'` option required by the [Fetch spec](https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-requestinit-duplex) for you in environments with [request stream support](https://caniuse.com/wf-fetch-request-streams).
1651| 
1652| ```js
1653| import ky from 'ky';
1654| 
1655| const stream = new ReadableStream({
1656| 	start(controller) {
1657| 		controller.enqueue(new TextEncoder().encode('hello'));
1658| 		controller.close();
1659| 	},
1660| });
1661| 
1662| const response = await ky.post('https://example.com/upload', {
1663| 	body: stream,
1664| });
1665| ```
1666| 
1667| > [!NOTE]
1668| > When retries are enabled (the default), Ky buffers the entire streaming body in memory to support replaying it. Set `retry: {limit: 0}` to skip this if retries aren't needed.
1669| 
1670| ### Consuming Server-Sent Events (SSE)
1671| 
1672| Use [`parse-sse`](https://github.com/sindresorhus/parse-sse):
1673| 
1674| ```js
1675| import ky from 'ky';
1676| import {parseServerSentEvents} from 'parse-sse';
1677| 
1678| const response = await ky('https://api.example.com/events');
1679| 
1680| for await (const event of parseServerSentEvents(response)) {
1681| 	console.log(event.data);
1682| }
1683| ```
1684| 
1685| ### Pagination
1686| 
1687| Use [`fetch-extras`](https://github.com/sindresorhus/fetch-extras) with Ky for paginating API responses:
1688| 
1689| ```js
1690| import ky from 'ky';
1691| import {paginate} from 'fetch-extras';
1692| 
1693| const url = 'https://api.github.com/repos/sindresorhus/ky/commits';
1694| 
1695| for await (const commit of paginate(url, {fetchFunction: ky})) {
1696| 	console.log(commit.sha);
1697| }
1698| ```
1699| 
1700| ### Extending types
1701| 
1702| Ky's TypeScript types are intentionally defined as type aliases rather than interfaces to prevent global module augmentation, which can lead to type conflicts and unexpected behavior across your codebase. If you need to add custom properties to Ky's types like `KyResponse` or `HTTPError`, create local wrapper types instead:
1703| 
1704| ```ts
1705| import ky, {HTTPError, isHTTPError} from 'ky';
1706| 
1707| interface CustomError extends HTTPError {
1708| 	customProperty: unknown;
1709| }
1710| 
1711| // Use with type assertion
1712| let data: unknown;
1713| 
1714| const api = ky.extend({
1715| 	hooks: {
1716| 		beforeError: [
1717| 			async ({error}) => {
1718| 				if (isHTTPError(error)) {
1719| 					(error as CustomError).customProperty = 'value';
1720| 					data = (error as CustomError).customProperty;
1721| 				}
1722| 
1723| 				return error;
1724| 			}
1725| 		]
1726| 	}
1727| });
1728| ```
1729| 
1730| This approach keeps your types scoped to where they're needed without polluting the global namespace.
1731| 
1732| ## FAQ
1733| 
1734| #### How do I use this in Node.js?
1735| 
1736| Node.js supports `fetch` natively, so you can just use this package directly.
1737| 
1738| #### How do I use this with a web app (React, Vue.js, etc.) that uses server-side rendering (SSR)?
1739| 
1740| Node.js supports `fetch` natively, so you can use Ky directly. The main consideration is that server-side requests require absolute URLs, while client-side requests can use relative URLs. Handle this with a conditional `baseUrl`:
1741| 
1742| ```js
1743| const api = ky.create({
1744| 	baseUrl: globalThis.window === undefined
1745| 		? (process.env.BASE_URL ?? 'http://localhost:3000')
1746| 		: undefined,
1747| });
1748| ```
1749| 
1750| <a name="baseurl-vs-prefix"></a>
1751| #### What's the difference between `baseUrl` and `prefix`?
1752| 
1753| **`baseUrl`** follows standard URL resolution rules — the same behavior as `new URL(input, baseUrl)`. A leading slash on the input means origin-root, overriding any path in the base URL.
1754| 
1755| **`prefix`** does plain string joining before URL resolution. The leading slash on the input is stripped, so it always appends to the prefix regardless.
1756| 
1757| Use `baseUrl` in almost all cases. Use `prefix` only when you want origin-relative inputs like `/users` to be treated as page-relative.
1758| 
1759| ```js
1760| // On https://example.com
1761| 
1762| // baseUrl: standard URL resolution
1763| ky('users',  {baseUrl: '/api/'});
1764| //=> https://example.com/api/users
1765| 
1766| ky('/users', {baseUrl: '/api/'});
1767| //=> https://example.com/users  ← leading slash wins
1768| 
1769| 
1770| // prefix: always appends
1771| ky('users',  {prefix: '/api'});
1772| //=> https://example.com/api/users
1773| 
1774| ky('/users', {prefix: '/api'});
1775| //=> https://example.com/api/users  ← leading slash ignored
1776| ```
1777| 
1778| #### How do I test a browser library that uses this?
1779| 
1780| Use a test runner that can run in the browser, like [Vitest](https://vitest.dev/guide/browser/) or [Playwright](https://playwright.dev).
1781| 
1782| #### How do I add authentication headers to every request?
1783| 
