1784| Use the [`beforeRequest` hook](#hooksbeforerequest) to attach headers before each request:
1785| 
1786| ```js
1787| const api = ky.create({
1788| 	hooks: {
1789| 		beforeRequest: [
1790| 			({request}) => {
1791| 				request.headers.set('Authorization', `Bearer ${getToken()}`);
1792| 			}
1793| 		]
1794| 	}
1795| });
1796| ```
1797| 
1798| #### How do I implement token refresh on 401 responses?
1799| 
1800| Configure `retry` to retry on 401, then use the [`beforeRetry` hook](#hooksbeforeretry) to refresh the token:
1801| 
1802| ```js
1803| const api = ky.create({
1804| 	retry: {statusCodes: [401]},
1805| 	hooks: {
1806| 		beforeRetry: [
1807| 			async ({request}) => {
1808| 				const token = await refreshToken();
1809| 				request.headers.set('Authorization', `Bearer ${token}`);
1810| 			}
1811| 		]
1812| 	}
1813| });
1814| ```
1815| 
1816| #### How do I mock Ky in tests?
1817| 
1818| Use [MSW](https://mswjs.io) to intercept requests at the network level without modifying your code. Alternatively, pass a custom `fetch` to your Ky instance:
1819| 
1820| ```js
1821| const api = ky.create({
1822| 	fetch: async () => new Response(JSON.stringify({name: 'Test'}), {
1823| 		headers: {
1824| 			'Content-Type': 'application/json'
1825| 		},
1826| 	}),
1827| });
1828| ```
1829| 
1830| #### How do I retry based on the response body?
1831| 
1832| Use [`ky.retry()`](#kyretryoptions) in an [`afterResponse`](#hooksafterresponse) hook. This lets you force a retry even when the status code is 2xx:
1833| 
1834| ```js
1835| import ky from 'ky';
1836| 
1837| const response = await ky('https://example.com', {
1838| 	hooks: {
1839| 		afterResponse: [
1840| 			async ({response}) => {
1841| 				const data = await response.json();
1842| 
1843| 				if (data.status === 'pending') {
1844| 					return ky.retry();
1845| 				}
1846| 			}
1847| 		]
1848| 	}
1849| });
1850| ```
1851| 
1852| The retry respects `retry.limit` and is observable in `beforeRetry` hooks.
1853| 
1854| #### How do I stop retrying early?
1855| 
1856| Throw from a `beforeRetry` hook to stop retrying and propagate the error, or return [`ky.stop`](#kystop) to stop silently (the request resolves with `undefined`):
1857| 
1858| ```js
1859| import ky, {isHTTPError} from 'ky';
1860| 
1861| const response = await ky('https://example.com', {
1862| 	hooks: {
1863| 		beforeRetry: [
1864| 			({error}) => {
1865| 				if (isHTTPError(error) && error.response.status === 400) {
1866| 					throw error; // Stop retrying, propagate the error
1867| 				}
1868| 			}
1869| 		]
1870| 	}
1871| });
1872| ```
1873| 
1874| #### How do I only throw on certain HTTP errors?
1875| 
1876| For simple status-based filtering, pass a function to `throwHttpErrors`:
1877| 
1878| ```js
1879| import ky from 'ky';
1880| 
1881| // Only throw on 5xx errors; 4xx responses are returned normally
1882| const api = ky.create({
1883| 	throwHttpErrors: status => status >= 500,
1884| });
1885| 
1886| const response = await api('https://example.com/resource');
1887| 
1888| if (response.status === 404) {
1889| 	// Handle "not found" as normal app flow
1890| }
1891| ```
1892| 
1893| If you need to access the response body in the non-throwing path, use `throwHttpErrors: false` and throw manually in an [`afterResponse`](#hooksafterresponse) hook:
1894| 
1895| ```js
1896| import ky, {HTTPError} from 'ky';
1897| 
1898| const api = ky.create({
1899| 	throwHttpErrors: false,
1900| 	hooks: {
1901| 		afterResponse: [
1902| 			({request, options, response}) => {
1903| 				if (response.status >= 500) {
1904| 					throw new HTTPError(response, request, options);
1905| 				}
1906| 			}
1907| 		]
1908| 	}
1909| });
1910| ```
1911| 
1912| #### How do I use this without a bundler like Webpack?
1913| 
1914| Make sure your code is running as a JavaScript module (ESM), for example by using a `<script type="module">` tag in your HTML document. Then Ky can be imported directly by that module without a bundler or other tools.
1915| 
1916| ```html
1917| <script type="module">
1918| import ky from 'https://unpkg.com/ky/distribution/index.js';
1919| 
1920| const json = await ky('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1').json();
1921| 
1922| console.log(json.title);
1923| //=> 'delectus aut autem'
1924| </script>
1925| ```
1926| 
1927| #### How is it different from [`got`](https://github.com/sindresorhus/got)?
1928| 
1929| Got is maintained by the same people as Ky, so you probably want Ky instead. It's smaller, works in the browser too, and is more stable since it's built on Fetch.
1930| 
1931| #### How is it different from [`axios`](https://github.com/axios/axios)?
1932| 
1933| Axios predates the Fetch API and has a lot of legacy baggage. Ky is built on Fetch, which means it's smaller, more standards-compliant, and works everywhere Fetch does (browsers, Node.js, Bun, Deno). Ky also has a more modern API with better TypeScript support.
1934| 
1935| #### What does `ky` mean?
1936| 
1937| It's just a random short npm package name I managed to get. It does, however, have a meaning in Japanese:
1938| 
1939| > A form of text-able slang, KY is an abbreviation for 空気読めない (kuuki yomenai), which literally translates into “cannot read the air.” It's a phrase applied to someone who misses the implied meaning.
1940| 
